IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `oapa-nouns`

-- Offences against the Person Act 1861 -- LAYER 1: ACTS CAUSING OR TENDING TO CAUSE
-- DANGER TO LIFE OR BODILY HARM, FIRST GROUP (sections 17 to 25): impeding a person
-- saving their life from shipwreck, wounding and grievous bodily harm, choking and
-- drugs to commit another offence, poison, and the alternative verdict.
--
-- Words the text uses but does not define, taken as facts the person states:
--   "unlawfully"  -- not authorised or excused by law.  The Act alone does not say what
--                    authorises or excuses; whether the person acted unlawfully is a
--                    fact they state.
--   "maliciously" -- the Act does not define it.  Whether the person acted maliciously
--                    is a fact they state.
--   "wound", "grievous bodily harm", "indictable offence" -- not defined here; stated.
-- The punishment words "penal servitude" and "imprisonment" are the text's own; where
-- the text's penalty clause reads "penal servitude . . ." the term is not stated and
-- the encoding says so (`for a term the text does not state`).

§ `Acts causing or tending to cause Danger to Life or Bodily Harm: sections 17 to 25`

§§ `Section 17: impeding a person endeavouring to save himself from shipwreck`

-- src:23 | 17 Impeding a person endeavouring to save himself from shipwreck.
-- src:24 | Whosoever shall unlawfully and maliciously prevent or impede any person, being on board of or having quitted any ship or vessel which shall be in distress, or wrecked, stranded, or cast on shore, in his endeavour to save his life, or shall unlawfully and maliciously prevent or impede any person in his endeavour to save the life of any such person as in this section first aforesaid, shall be guilty of felony, and being convicted thereof shall be liable . . . to be kept in penal servitude for life . . .
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 17 (src:23-24)
-- Two limbs: (1) preventing or impeding a person who is on board a ship or vessel in
-- distress, wrecked, stranded or cast on shore (or who has left it) in their own
-- endeavour to save their life; (2) preventing or impeding a person in their endeavour
-- to save the life of such a person.
DECLARE `Conduct under section 17` HAS
    `unlawfully` IS A BOOLEAN
    `maliciously` IS A BOOLEAN
    `prevented or impeded a person on board or who had quitted a ship or vessel in distress, wrecked, stranded or cast on shore, in their endeavour to save their life` IS A BOOLEAN
    `prevented or impeded a person in their endeavour to save the life of such a person` IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 17 (src:24)
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct under section 17`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 17: impeding a person endeavouring to save their life from shipwreck` c MEANS
        c's `unlawfully`
    AND c's `maliciously`
    AND (   c's `prevented or impeded a person on board or who had quitted a ship or vessel in distress, wrecked, stranded or cast on shore, in their endeavour to save their life`
         OR c's `prevented or impeded a person in their endeavour to save the life of such a person`)

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 17 (src:24)
GIVETH A Classification
`s 17: what the text calls the offence` MEANS felony

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 17 (src:24)
GIVETH A LIST OF Sentence
`s 17: the sentences the text states` MEANS
    LIST `penal servitude` `for life`

§§ `Section 18: shooting or attempting to shoot, or wounding with intent to do grievous bodily harm`

-- src:25 | 18 Shooting or attempting to shoot, or wounding with intent to do grievous bodily harm.
-- src:26 | Whosoever shall unlawfully and maliciously by any means whatsoever wound or cause any grievous bodily harm to any person, . . . with intent, . . . to do some . . . grievous bodily harm to any person, or with intent to resist or prevent the lawful apprehension or detainer of any person, shall be guilty of felony, and being convicted thereof shall be liable . . . to be kept in penal servitude for life . . .
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 18 (src:25-26)
-- Conduct: "wound or cause any grievous bodily harm to any person", "by any means
-- whatsoever".  Intent, either of two: "to do some . . . grievous bodily harm to any
-- person", or "to resist or prevent the lawful apprehension or detainer of any person"
-- (any person: not necessarily the person wounded).
DECLARE `Conduct under section 18` HAS
    `unlawfully` IS A BOOLEAN
    `maliciously` IS A BOOLEAN
    `wounded a person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `caused grievous bodily harm to a person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `intended to do some grievous bodily harm to a person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `intended to resist or prevent the lawful apprehension or detainer of a person` IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 18 (src:26)
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct under section 18`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 18: wounding or causing grievous bodily harm, with intent` c MEANS
        c's `unlawfully`
    AND c's `maliciously`
    AND (   c's `wounded a person`
         OR c's `caused grievous bodily harm to a person`)
    AND (   c's `intended to do some grievous bodily harm to a person`
         OR c's `intended to resist or prevent the lawful apprehension or detainer of a person`)

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 18 (src:26)
GIVETH A Classification
`s 18: what the text calls the offence` MEANS felony

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 18 (src:26)
GIVETH A LIST OF Sentence
`s 18: the sentences the text states` MEANS
    LIST `penal servitude` `for life`

