    LIST `penal servitude` `for a term the text does not state`

§§ `Section 25: if the jury be not satisfied that any person charged is guilty of felony, but guilty of misdemeanor, they may find him guilty accordingly`

-- src:38 | 25 If the jury be not satisfied that any person charged is guilty of felony, but guilty of misdemeanor they may find him guilty accordingly.
-- src:39 | If, upon the trial of any person for any felony in the last but one preceding section mentioned, the jury shall not be satisfied that such person is guilty thereof, but shall be satisfied that he is guilty of any misdemeanor in the last preceding section mentioned, then and in every such case the jury may acquit the accused of such felony, and find him guilty of such misdemeanor, and thereupon he shall be liable to be punished in the same manner as if convicted upon an indictment for such misdemeanor.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 25 (src:38-39)
-- "the last but one preceding section" is section 23 and "the last preceding section"
-- is section 24.  What the jury is "satisfied" of is the jury's judgment, taken as a
-- fact about the trial (the jury's state of mind is not the accused's to state).
DECLARE `Trial under section 25` HAS
    `the person is tried for the felony in section 23` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the jury is not satisfied that the person is guilty of that felony` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the jury is satisfied that the person is guilty of the misdemeanor in section 24` IS A BOOLEAN

-- "the jury may acquit the accused of such felony, and find him guilty of such
-- misdemeanor": a power of the jury and a liability of the accused.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 25 (src:39)
GIVEN t IS A `Trial under section 25`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 25: the jury may acquit of the section 23 felony and find the person guilty of the section 24 misdemeanor` t MEANS
        t's `the person is tried for the felony in section 23`
    AND t's `the jury is not satisfied that the person is guilty of that felony`
    AND t's `the jury is satisfied that the person is guilty of the misdemeanor in section 24`

-- "thereupon he shall be liable to be punished in the same manner as if convicted upon
-- an indictment for such misdemeanor": the section 24 sentences, and none otherwise.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 25 (src:39)
GIVEN t IS A `Trial under section 25`
GIVETH A LIST OF Sentence
`s 25: the sentences open after an alternative verdict` t MEANS
    IF `s 25: the jury may acquit of the section 23 felony and find the person guilty of the section 24 misdemeanor` t
    THEN `s 24: the sentences the text states`
    ELSE EMPTY
